---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CaseStudyHeader from '../../components/notebook/CaseStudyHeader.astro';
import CaseHero from '../../components/notebook/CaseHero.astro';
import SectionRow from '../../components/notebook/SectionRow.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/notebook/Figure.astro';

import gbm1 from '../../assets/data-science-club/gbm-llms.png';
import gbm2 from '../../assets/data-science-club/gbm-pytorch.png';
import gbm3 from '../../assets/data-science-club/gbm-3.png';
import gbm4 from '../../assets/data-science-club/gbm-4.png';
import gbm5 from '../../assets/data-science-club/gbm-5.png';
import gbm6 from '../../assets/data-science-club/gbm-6.png';
import mascot from '../../assets/data-science-club/mascot-agents.png';
---

<Base title="CMU Data Science Club · VK">
  <div style="background: var(--paper); min-height: 100vh; color: var(--ink); padding-bottom: 120px">

    <CaseStudyHeader entryNum="" />

    <CaseHero
      eyebrow="CMU Data Science Club"
      title="Design something the next board can reuse"
      lede="Two years as Design Director: brand identity, event graphics, mascots, and the website for the first Poker AI competition at CMU, which the next board built on the following year."
      meta={[
        { label: "Role", value: "Design Director" },
        { label: "Timeline", value: "Dec 2023 – Dec 2025" },
        { label: "Tools", value: "Figma, Canva, HTML / CSS / JS" },
        { label: "Output", value: "DSC brand system, Poker AI 2024 site, event graphics" },
      ]}
      note="handed: design the club identity and the competition site.<br><br>turned out: design something the next board can reuse."
    />

    <SectionRow num="01" label="the role">
      <Fragment slot="main">
        <p class="body">
          CMU's Data Science Club runs workshops, speaker events, and competitions across the year.
          As Design Director I owned everything visual: the logo, the mascots, GBM and workshop
          graphics, speaker cards, and the website for the club's first Poker AI competition,
          co-hosted with the Poker Club and backed by Jane Street, HRT, Jump Trading, SIG, IMC,
          and Trexquant.
        </p>
        <p class="body">
          The event ran over 24 hours. Teams of up to four built bots to play a poker variant
          that wasn't revealed until the start. No poker knowledge required, workshops provided,
          $8,000+ prize pool.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="margin">
        <p class="note" style="margin:0;transform:rotate(-1deg)">
          the brief for every individual event: make it look like this event is worth showing up
          for, and make it easy for the next person to update.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
    </SectionRow>

    <SectionRow num="02" label="the system">
      <Fragment slot="main">
        <p class="body">
          Consistency is what makes a student org look credible to sponsors. The DSC logo is a
          tree-of-data mark, branches ending in nodes, designed to work in navy on white and white
          on dark. It sits in the same corner of every graphic. Type hierarchy and proportions stay
          fixed. Inside that frame, each event type gets its own mood: workshops use playful
          line-drawn people; speaker cards use soft gradient portraits; competitions go bold on black
          with a mascot.
        </p>
        <p class="body">
          The Poker AI 2024 site was built in HTML, CSS, and JS, specifically so the next board
          could update it without a designer. They did. The 2025 splash page was built directly
          on mine.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="margin">
        <p class="note" style="margin:0;transform:rotate(0.6deg)">
          monospace type throughout the site: the audience is programmers. it says "this is a
          coding event" before you read a word.
          <br><br>
          <a href="https://cmudsc.com/archive/pokerai-2024" class="note-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Poker AI 2024 site →</a>
        </p>
      </Fragment>
    </SectionRow>

    <Figure
      fig="fig. 1"
      wide
      caption="GBM and workshop event graphics. Each event gets its own mood inside a consistent frame: corner logo, fixed type hierarchy, event-specific illustration style."
      note="six events, six different vibes, same system underneath."
      images={[
        { src: gbm1, alt: "Intro to LLMs Workshop poster: blue gradient with DSC logo, monospace type, phone graphic.", label: "" },
        { src: gbm2, alt: "PyTorch Workshop poster: orange serif italic heading, hand-drawn stick figures at laptops.", label: "" },
        { src: gbm3, alt: "DSC event graphic 3.", label: "" },
        { src: gbm4, alt: "DSC event graphic 4.", label: "" },
        { src: gbm5, alt: "DSC event graphic 5.", label: "" },
        { src: gbm6, alt: "DSC event graphic 6.", label: "" },
      ]}
      ratio="1 / 1"
    />

    <SectionRow num="03" label="the mascots">
      <Fragment slot="main">
        <p class="body">
          Each competition got its own robot character. The AI Agents Weekend mascot is a blue
          sprout-robot: rounded, friendly, clearly a bot. The Poker AI mascot has card suits for
          eyes. They're designed to make a technical event feel approachable, the same way the
          Poker AI site answered "no poker knowledge needed" in the first scroll.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="margin">
        <p class="note" style="margin:0;transform:rotate(-0.6deg)">
          same "can I do this?" problem as Girls on the Run. different audience, same design answer:
          say it plainly and put a friendly face on it.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
    </SectionRow>

    <Figure
      fig="fig. 2"
      caption="AI Agents Weekend mascot. Blue sprout-robot, designed to make a DeepMind-and-Google-Cloud event feel like something a first-year could walk into."
      note="i drew these. the sprout is a nod to the DSC tree-of-data logo."
      images={[
        { src: mascot, alt: "Blue sprout-robot mascot: rounded body with a smiling screen face and two leaves sprouting from the top." },
      ]}
      ratio="4 / 5"
    />

    <footer class="cs-row" style="padding-top: 24px; align-items: end">
      <div></div>
      <a href="/design" class="back mono">← Design</a>
      <div></div>
    </footer>

  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .body {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 21px;
    line-height: 1.62;
    margin: 0 0 22px;
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 20, 'wght' 400;
    max-width: none;
  }
  .body:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .note-link { color: var(--pencil); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; }
  .back { color: var(--ink-60); text-decoration: none; font-size: 13px; }
  .back:hover { color: var(--ink); }
  @media (max-width: 1000px) { .body { font-size: 19px; } }
</style>
